WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.237
This query looks almost identical to the previous one, but the meaning changes because the Glucose condition

00:00:08.237 --> 00:00:08.592
moved from ON to WHERE.

00:00:08.592 --> 00:00:12.142
The LEFT JOIN first creates a result that preserves patients.

00:00:12.142 --> 00:00:16.672
For patients without lab matches, the lab columns are NULL.

00:00:16.672 --> 00:00:20.002
Then the WHERE clause filters those joined rows.

00:00:20.002 --> 00:00:26.262
When it asks whether test_name equals Glucose, NULL rows do not satisfy the condition,

00:00:26.262 --> 00:00:29.047
so the unmatched patients disappear.

00:00:29.047 --> 00:00:34.237
This is a classic SQL trap because the query is syntactically correct.

00:00:34.237 --> 00:00:36.422
PostgreSQL will not warn you.

00:00:36.422 --> 00:00:42.422
The only way to catch the problem is to understand the intended population and validate the output.

00:00:42.422 --> 00:00:46.442
A good debugging method is to run both versions side by side.

00:00:46.442 --> 00:00:49.167
Count the distinct patients in each result.

00:00:49.167 --> 00:00:54.317
Ask which result matches the business requirement.

00:00:54.317 --> 00:00:59.127
In real analytics work, this difference can affect denominators, percentages, risk calculations,

00:00:59.127 --> 00:01:06.197
and dashboards. If the requirement says all patients, but your WHERE clause removes unmatched patients,

00:01:06.197 --> 00:01:10.865
the analysis can be systematically biased without producing any error message.
